==English==

===Alternative forms===
* [[attonce]] {{qualifier|obsolete}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|en|at}} + {{m|en|once}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ət ˈwʌns/|lang=en}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|head=[[at]] [[once]]|-}}

# {{context|obsolete|lang=en}} In [[one]] group; [[together]].
#* '''1590''', {{w|Edmund Spenser}}, ''{{w|The Faerie Queene}}'', III.4:
#*: Those two great champions did '''attonce''' pursew / The fearefull damzell with incessany payns [...].
# {{context|idiomatic|lang=en}} [[at the same time|At the same time]]; [[simultaneously]].
#: {{usex|Can you pat your head and rub your belly '''at once'''?<br />He tried to eat four cookies '''at once.'''|lang=en}}
#* {{quote-magazine|author=Lexington
|title=Keeping the mighty honest: A new wave of press barons should not allow newspapers to become niche products|url=http://www.economist.com/news/united-states/21583274-new-wave-press-barons-should-not-allow-newspapers-become-niche-products-keeping|magazine={{w|The Economist}}
|date=10 August 2013|volume=408|issue=8848|passage=The [''Washington''] ''Post's'' proprietor through those turbulent &#91;{{w|Watergate}}&#93; days, Katharine Graham, held a double place in Washington’s hierarchy: '''at once''' regal Georgetown hostess and scrappy newshound, ready to hold the establishment to account.}}
#* {{quote-news|author=Tom Payne|title=Sapiens: a Brief History of Humankind by Yuval Noah Harari, review: 'urgent questions' &#91;print version: The story of our species, 27 September 2014, p.&nbsp;R32&#93;|url=http://www.telegraph.co.uk/culture/books/bookreviews/11120947/Sapiens-a-Brief-History-of-Humankind-by-Yuval-Noah-Harari-review-urgent-questions.html|work={{w|The Daily Telegraph}} (Review)|date=26 September 2014|passage=[T]he book, constructed in short, lucid episodes, can be satisfyingly read as a sequence of provocative talks, '''at once''' well informed and vatic.}}
# {{context|idiomatic|lang=en}} [[immediately|Immediately]]; [[now]]; [[right away]].
#: {{usex|Tell the doctor to come '''at once'''. She is having a baby.|lang=en}}
#* {{quote-book|year=1898|author={{w|Winston Churchill (novelist)|Winston Churchill}}
|title={{w|The Celebrity}}|chapter=4
|passage=Mr. Cooke '''at once''' began a tirade against the residents of Asquith for permitting a sandy and generally disgraceful condition of the roads. So roundly did he vituperate the inn management in particular, and with such a loud flow of words, that I trembled lest he should be heard on the veranda.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=1918|author={{w|W. B. Maxwell}}|chapter=3
|title=[http://openlibrary.org/works/OL1097634W The Mirror and the Lamp]
|passage=One saint's day in mid-term a certain newly appointed suffragan-bishop came to the school chapel, and there preached on “The Inner Life.”&nbsp; He '''at once''' secured attention by his informal method, and when presently the coughing of Jarvis […] interrupted the sermon, he altogether captivated his audience with a remark about cough lozenges being cheap and easily procurable.}}
#* {{quote-book|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=19|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=As soon as Julia returned with a constable, Timothy, who was on the point of exhaustion, prepared to give over to him gratefully. The newcomer turned out to be a powerful youngster, fully trained and eager to help, and he stripped off his tunic '''at once'''.}}

